\section{Introduction}
Accurate modeling of multi-body shadow geometry requires simultaneous handling of 
relativistic ephemerides, high-resolution flux fields, and geometric boundary 
surfaces. Existing astrophysical solvers operate at limited spatial scales or 
lack real-time performance. UPAS-X addresses these limitations through a unified 
architecture combining high-order numerical integration, analytic geometry, and 
massively parallel GPU computation.

%------------------------------------------------------------
% SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE
%------------------------------------------------------------

\section{System Architecture}
UPAS-X operates across 16 enterprise compute nodes equipped with 128 NVIDIA H100 
GPUs interconnected via NVSwitch and NVLink 4.0. Each node contains dual AMD EPYC 
9654 CPUs and 1.5 TB DDR5-4800 RAM. MPI distributes spatial tensors exceeding 
$10^9$ nodes, while CUDA kernels perform ray--cone intersection and BVH traversal.

%------------------------------------------------------------
% METHODS
%------------------------------------------------------------

\section{Methods}

\subsection{Ephemeris Integration}
Orbital trajectories were computed using a 15th-order Runge--Kutta--Nyström 
symplectic integrator with adaptive step-size control. Hamiltonian drift was 
monitored continuously:
\[
\left| \frac{H(t)-H(0)}{H(0)} \right| < 10^{-10}.
\]

\subsection{BVH Construction and Traversal}
A Surface Area Heuristic (SAH) binned builder generated the BVH hierarchy. 
Dynamic motion triggered GPU-based refit kernels. BVH traversal depth and 
rebuild frequency were logged for stability analysis.

\subsection{Ray--Cone Intersection}
Umbra, penumbra, and antumbra boundaries were computed using analytic cone 
geometry. CUDA kernels evaluated intersection distances and flux attenuation 
for each ray.

\subsection{Flux Modeling}
Stellar flux fields were generated using Gaia DR3 and Kepler limb-darkening 
coefficients. HDR flux linearity was validated across shadow zones.

\subsection{Validation Datasets}
\begin{itemize}
    \item NASA GSFC eclipse maps (2017-08-21, 2024-04-08)
    \item NASA JPL HORIZONS ephemerides (DE440/DE441)
    \item Gaia DR3 / Kepler photometric catalogs
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Performance Measurement}
Rays/sec throughput, frame latency, VRAM utilization, and BVH traversal depth 
were recorded under increasing spatial node counts and source densities.

\subsection{Reproducibility}
Deterministic execution was enforced using SHA-256 seed locking, immutable JSON 
configuration manifests, and semantic versioning across all simulation campaigns.

%------------------------------------------------------------
% RESULTS
%------------------------------------------------------------

\section{Results}

\subsection{Eclipse Reconstruction}
Historical eclipses were simulated and compared against NASA GSFC maps. Timing 
residuals were computed as:
\[
\Delta t = t_{\text{UPAS-X}} - t_{\text{GSFC}}.
\]

\subsection{Ephemeris Cross-Checks}
Trajectories were validated against JPL HORIZONS (DE440/DE441). Position residuals:
\[
\Delta r = \| r_{\text{UPAS-X}} - r_{\text{HORIZONS}} \|.
\]

\subsection{Performance}
UPAS-X achieved 1.42 billion rays/sec and maintained frame latency below 
16.6 ms under dense star-field conditions. VRAM utilization approached 98.4\% 
across all GPUs.

